ApeIn started as an NFT collection and grew into Isle of Motu, a cross-chain GameFi world with three titles. I was co-founder and creative director. The Figma files run from February 2021 to December 2023.

When I say I want to be known for "seeing the whole system: the creative concept, the economic model, the technical execution," ApeIn is the example I mean.

{% Figure src="/images/projects/apein/welcome-to-ape-island.webp" alt="Title screen: a wooden sign reading Welcome To Ape Island under an Expansion Season ribbon, with a 3D ape in a knit-textured head and an Enter Ape Island button, over a dark island backdrop" caption="The title screen for the Ape Island expansion season." width="wide" /%}

## Rolling an ape

The center of it was the Ape Maker. You don't buy a fixed picture. You roll an ape.

On the roll screen a 3D ape stands in the middle. Each body part (head, arms, legs, torso) rolled on its own and shows a star level from one to three and a material, like Wooly, Tinker or Galactic. The parts add up to a rarity for the whole ape. Under it are two choices: buy the ape in front of you, or hit "Make Another?" and spend credits on a new roll.

{% Figure src="/images/projects/apein/ape-maker-roll.webp" alt="The Ape Maker roll screen: a 3D ape in the center, each body part labeled with one to three stars and a material such as Wooly, Tinker or Galactic, a stats panel on the left, a rarity dial bottom left, and Make Another and Buy Now buttons" caption="One roll. Each part shows its own stars and material, and together they set the ape's rarity. Credit and rarity figures are blurred until I confirm they weren't mock values." width="wide" /%}

## 3D parts, rendered in the browser

I taught myself Blender to make the parts. They're modeled in Blender, the app picks them at random, and the assembled ape renders in the browser. As far as I know, it was the first design system built to render randomized 3D NFTs in the browser. The first version of that design system is dated March 2021, a month after the first Ape Maker flow.

{% Gallery images=[{src: "/images/projects/apein/sol-far-out-moss-ape.webp", alt: "A rendered 3D ape with a mossy head and white fur legs on a green and blue sunburst"}, {src: "/images/projects/apein/sol-lumbering-virgil-ready.webp", alt: "A rendered 3D ape in a galaxy material, purple and blue with stars, next to the text Lumbering Virgil is ready for the Summer of Love"}] columns="2" caption="Two rolled apes rendered from the parts: moss and galactic materials." /%}

## No game experience, $100K, nine months

When ApeIn turned into Isle of Motu, I'd never made a game. I had three months to learn, hire a team and plan, then six months to build, on a $100K budget. We shipped a Unity game in that window. One of the three titles, Jungle Rumble, was a tokenized PVP idle brawler.

The look was a cartoon tropical island with pirates, wooden-sign UI and a mascot who explains things.

